WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:14.080
The Egyptian pyramids are a monument to human&nbsp;
engineering, preserving both culture and mummies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:20.240
across millennia. But for the purposes of our&nbsp;
microcosmic journey, what’s most remarkable about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:28.160
them are the bits of fossils that made up their&nbsp;
limestone. As far back as the 5th century BCE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:34.480
the Greek historian Herodotus noticed&nbsp;
these shells embedded in the pyramids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:40.000
What he did not know is that these shells&nbsp;
were the remnants of creatures that are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:47.040
as intriguing as the pyramid’s own inhabitants.
In the early 19th century, far removed from those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:53.120
pyramids, a young boy named Alcide d’Orbigny&nbsp;
would become fascinated with the tiny shells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.360
he found on the beach. And when he grew up and&nbsp;
expanded his hobby into a more professional study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.640
he named them and the organisms they&nbsp;
came from. He called them foraminifera.&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.120
If you judge them just from appearances, it might&nbsp;
be a surprise to learn that foraminifera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.160
is an amoeboid protist, meaning that&nbsp;
inside that hard shell--which is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.760
called a test--there is a squishy amoeba.
While we don’t know too much about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.960
foraminifera evolved from other eukaryotes,&nbsp;
we know that they’ve been around for over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:36.560
500 million years. And over that time, they’ve&nbsp;
evolved at least 10,000 species that are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.120
living today and another 40,000 species&nbsp;
that we know only through their fossils.&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.480
There are two different main types of&nbsp;
foraminifera. Most of the living species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.440
we know are benthic foraminifera, which means&nbsp;
they’re found throughout the ocean’s depths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.360
living on rocks and seaweed or just the ocean&nbsp;
floor. They’ve even been found in abundance in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.120
the Challenger Deep, the deepest known point in&nbsp;
the ocean. The second group are the planktonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.360
foraminifera, which float on or near the surface&nbsp;
of the ocean thanks in part to their tests.&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.160
Both types of foraminifera make and inhabit&nbsp;
tests. And scientists can use the materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:22.000
and test morphology to distinguish between&nbsp;
different species. Some foraminifera secrete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.960
compounds like calcium carbonate, while&nbsp;
others glue bits of inorganic particles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:34.000
together with the organic equivalent of cement.
And inside, the tests are divided into chambers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.600
with some species creating complex structures out&nbsp;
of these chambers the way we might create complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:47.120
arrangements of rooms in a building. Foraminifera&nbsp;
are architects, and as they grow, they build more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:53.680
chambers to accommodate themselves. Some grow&nbsp;
so fast they have to add a chamber every day.&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.960
The shapes of these chambers play a particularly&nbsp;
crucial role for planktonic foraminifera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.000
which generally build less complex structures&nbsp;
compared to their benthic counterparts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.200
The chambers they build are more globular,&nbsp;
which provides the planktonic foraminifera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:18.320
with a means to stay buoyant as they float around.
But foraminifera, or “forams” as they call them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:26.880
the biz, don’t just build shells, they also build&nbsp;
webs. Or more accurately, they become webs.The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:33.040
foraminifera tests are lined with holes that&nbsp;
act like little windows to allow the organism’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:39.040
pseudopodia, or “false feet” to peek out.
And “peeking” might be an understatement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:44.720
The pseudopodia reach and reach out,&nbsp;
forming their own webs as they do to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:51.200
food and catch it. These forms of pseudopodia&nbsp;
are called reticulopodia, and you can see here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:58.640
just how weird and extensive they are in action.
They may look like a disintegrated spider’s web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:06.320
but remember that this is the organism&nbsp;
reaching its own body out to the microcosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.800
spreading itself thin as it contorts into&nbsp;
new shapes. Zoom in on those reticulopodium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.760
and you can see the cytoplasm streaming&nbsp;
through it like a biological highway.&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.520
When forams die, the soft matter that makes&nbsp;
up those pseudopodia will eventually decay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:31.520
but what remains are the tests. And over the&nbsp;
hundreds of millions of years they’ve existed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:37.600
the death of foraminifera has become&nbsp;
integrated into our planet’s geology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:44.800
Large swathes of the ocean floor are covered in&nbsp;
the collected remains of planktonic foraminifera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.880
creating a dense substance called Globigerina&nbsp;
ooze. And we owe famous sights like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:57.440
pink-tinted beaches of Bermuda and the nummulitic&nbsp;
limestone of the Pyramids to ancient foraminifera.&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.480
And the abundance of these fossilized remains&nbsp;
provides us with a record of our planet’s history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.520
While forams may be found throughout our oceans,&nbsp;
individual species have their own environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:15.360
needs, whether that means they prefer a particular&nbsp;
temperature or salinity or light condition. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.880
assessing the fossil composition of different&nbsp;
sediments helps scientists understand what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:27.040
our world once looked like. And understanding&nbsp;
the past shapes our present as well, with oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.600
companies using microfossils to understand&nbsp;
the geology of the area they’re working in.&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.480
So where did our fossils come from? Well, they&nbsp;
had a bit of a journey. James, our master of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:44.400
microscopes, got these samples from Virginia by&nbsp;
way of his fiancée, who collected water and sand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.440
from the coast and packed them in a jar.
If that seems like an easy enough way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.400
to give the lovely gift of microbes to a&nbsp;
microscopist, just remember that she had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:00.160
to take the samples from Virginia to Poland.&nbsp;
And that’s where things started to go wrong.&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:06.320
First the jar of sand and water leaked all&nbsp;
over her luggage. Then after she arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:13.360
James’ cat tipped over the jar, as cats do like&nbsp;
to do. But we cannot blame the cat for all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.480
subsequent damage either because James also&nbsp;
tipped the jar over while trying to open a window.&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.600
On the one hand, this story seems like something&nbsp;
of a cautionary tale on the importance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.240
careful jar handling. But even after&nbsp;
having lost all that water, the jar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.840
was still full of sand—and also what turned out&nbsp;
to be a whole bunch of foraminifera fossils.&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:39.120
And maybe that’s the true lesson to&nbsp;
take from our shelled, amoeboid friends:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:45.360
that even when the world around them has&nbsp;
tipped and turned and leaked, even then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.840
you can find something dazzling in what remains.&nbsp;

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.960
